\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and requirements engineering.}
Ambiguity has been studied extensively in both computational linguistics and software engineering. \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} propose an 11-type NLP taxonomy covering syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic ambiguity, from which we draw our linguistic-layer types. In requirements engineering (RE), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014} classify six ambiguity types in regulatory texts, and \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} introduce the distinction between nocuous ambiguity (where stakeholders diverge on interpretation) and innocuous ambiguity (where they converge). Computational detection of nocuous ambiguity has progressed from heuristic methods \citep{chantree_2006,willis_nocuous_2008} through NLP-based cross-domain detectors \citep{ferrari_ambiguity_2019,ferrari_defects_2018} to transformer-based approaches for anaphoric ambiguity \citep{ezzini_taphsir_2022,ezzini_anaphoric_2022}. \citet{zhao_nlp4re_2021} survey the broader NLP4RE landscape. Our work extends Berry and Kamsties' framework to LLM agents, where the absence of shared organizational context makes previously innocuous ambiguity nocuous.

\paragraph{Psycholinguistics of ambiguity processing.}
\citet{ferreira_good_enough_2002} show that human comprehenders build shallow, ``good-enough'' representations rather than full parses, a finding that parallels our observation that LLM agents adopt surface interpretations of ambiguous policy clauses. \citet{frazier_rayner_1982} demonstrate that readers commit to a single syntactic parse early and backtrack upon disambiguation failure, while \citet{levy_expectation_2008} formalizes this as expectation-based processing. \citet{li_garden_path_llm_2024} provide direct evidence that LLMs exhibit human-like garden-path effects, and \citet{poesio_artstein_2005} formalize genuine ambiguity in anaphoric annotation as inherent rather than resolvable. \citet{lassiter_goodman_2017} model vague adjective interpretation in a Bayesian framework. \citet{liu_ambiguity_2023} demonstrate that language models generally fail to represent ambiguity, collapsing multiple valid interpretations into a single output. These findings ground our ``Ambiguity Blindness'' result: agents, like humans, process ambiguous input without meta-cognitive awareness, but unlike humans, they lack the social and organizational channels to recover.

\paragraph{Agent safety evaluation.}
The agent safety evaluation landscape includes benchmarks for risk awareness \citep{rjudge_2024}, tool-use safety \citep{toolemu_2024}, indirect prompt injection \citep{injecagent_2024}, and policy-invisible violations \citep{phantompolicy_2026}. \citet{shieldagent_2025} extract formal safety rules from policy documents, and AgentAuditor \citep{agentauditor_2025} achieves human-level safety evaluation. Our work differs by focusing on the \emph{source specification} rather than the agent's execution: we measure how ambiguity in the policy itself causes safety violations, complementing work that assumes clean policies.

\paragraph{Policy specification for agents.}
DRIP-R \citep{drip_r_2026} is the closest concurrent work: it benchmarks 400 policy-ambiguous retail scenarios across five RE ambiguity types, measuring decision quality rather than safety violations. Our work differs in two respects: (1) our matched-pair design establishes causality rather than correlating ambiguity presence with decision quality; (2) we quantify the model$\times$type interaction, revealing that authorization scope serves as a model-differentiating canary type. PolicyBank \citep{policybank_2026} studies iterative policy refinement, closing 82\% of the gap to a human oracle. AgentPex \citep{agentpex_2026} detects procedural violations in agentic traces, finding that 83\% of perfect-outcome traces contain violations. MANTRA \citep{mantra_2026} synthesizes SMT-validated compliance benchmarks from procedural manuals. $\tau$-bench \citep{tau_bench_2024} and its successor $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025} provide the conversational agent evaluation platforms.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background). Each annotator independently verified the three quality criteria; rejected or modified pairs were iteratively revised until both annotators confirmed compliance. For each type, we identify policy clauses in $\tau^2$-bench that are naturally susceptible to the target ambiguity, then construct the ambiguous and unambiguous variants. All pairs are reviewed to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target ambiguity type, (2) the unambiguous variant eliminates that ambiguity without introducing new information that changes the policy's scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural (would occur in real policy documents). A post-hoc realism validation confirms that ambiguous clauses are linguistically consistent with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models spanning three providers and two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, Qwen3, and DeepSeek traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 traces. We acknowledge this creates an asymmetry (a mid-tier model judging a frontier model in one direction), which we discuss in \S\ref{sec:limitations}. Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. Our primary analysis uses moderate-or-above severity as the violation threshold; minor-only deviations (6.6\% of episodes) are excluded. Including minor violations (overall rate 49.6\% vs.\ 41.0\%) produces even larger effects without changing any directional conclusion.

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm that the observed effect is specific to semantic ambiguity rather than general prompt sensitivity, we run 120 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations: synonym substitution (replacing content words with near-synonyms) and clause reordering (shuffling the order of policy sub-clauses). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = 3.33$ pp (95\% CI $[-5.0, 13.3]$, containing zero), compared to the ambiguity effect of $\Delta_{\text{ambig}} = 34.0$ pp. A post-hoc sensitivity analysis indicates that with $n = 120$ episodes at $\alpha = 0.05$ and 80\% power, the design can detect perturbation effects $\geq 15$ pp, sufficient to rule out effects approaching the ambiguity magnitude but not small perturbation effects.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} To verify that violation rates are stable across independent runs, we replicate 30 clause pairs (selected via stratified random sampling, 5 per type) $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates. The intraclass correlation coefficient is ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$), indicating excellent reproducibility.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus type and model effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017}, and bootstrap 95\% CIs (10{,}000 resamples). TOST uses $\pm 15$ pp as the primary margin (one additional violation per seven episodes); we provide sensitivity analyses across $\pm 8$--$20$ pp. Holm--Bonferroni correction is applied within three families: (i) six per-type Fisher tests, (ii) five per-model McNemar tests, and (iii) fifteen pairwise type comparisons. C3's individual $p = 0.017$ does not survive correction within the four-claim family, consistent with its classification as inconclusive.

